00:00:56.394 --> 00:00:58.360
So before I jump
into the actual demo,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.430
I just wanted to cover
sort of the architecture

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.555
that we'll be representing
through this demo today.

00:01:02.555 --> 00:01:04.959
We are generating random
data through Compute Engine

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.720
and feeding it into a Pub/Sub
topic, which we will then

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.360
use Cloud Dataflow to stream
it into BigQuery in real time.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.785
Let's go ahead and just
jump into the actual demo.

00:01:13.785 --> 00:01:15.160
So the first thing
I want to show

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.660
about how I got
this set up is I'm

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.270
going to head over to
Google Cloud Storage, which

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.270
I mentioned is how we did
the device simulations

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.690
and also where we created
some folders for our Dataflow

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.370
pipeline.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:31.480
So we created a bucket
titled IoT Airlines v1 data.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.442
We created two folders
within this bucket.

00:01:33.442 --> 00:01:35.400
And so that's going to
include some information

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.639
about our device
simulators here.

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:39.180
And we've also
created another bucket

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.322
for our Dataflow pipeline.

00:01:41.322 --> 00:01:42.780
So for our Dataflow
pipeline, we've

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.650
also included some files here.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.650
And we created two folders
for staging and temp.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.399
It includes spinning
up and shutting down,

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.445
Google Compute Engine
instances, and the access

00:01:50.445 --> 00:01:54.350
these GSA buckets for both I/O
and temporary file staging.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:02:00.320
Now, we've also set up
a Pub/Sub subscription.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.750
So the first thing we do
here is head over to Pub/Sub

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.550
and you see here
we've created a topic

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.174
and this topic is titled
"Airline Messages."

00:02:07.174 --> 00:02:09.340
The type of subscription
we created under this topic

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.100
is a pool subscription.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.770
And we've named
it Dataflow here.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.830
So you can see here a publisher
application, in this case,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.420
would be our IoT simulators.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.550
They create a topic in
Pub/Sub, which we've

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.990
titled "Airline Messages."

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.570
And that's a service that
sends messages to the topic.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.320
Now, going back a little bit,
I'm going to show you IoT Core

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.780
because this is also the
component in which we register

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.340
our IoT simulators.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.220
What we've done here is we've
registered this right here

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.870
called IoT airlines registry.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.900
We've essentially cloned
the git repository,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.954
where we have java files.

00:02:41.954 --> 00:02:43.620
And we've compiled
the application here,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.160
upload a JAR file to the storage
bucket that we showed earlier.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.870
This is our registry ID.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:53.600
It's going to accept in the
form of MQTT and HTTP protocols

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.890
in the region US central one.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.380
We have Pub/Sub set up for
this IoT device registration,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.860
which is the one we created
earlier, Airline Messages.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.200
Anytime you register a device
to connect to IoT Core,

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.300
you just need to register
it first with Cloud IoT.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.220
It consists of adding a
device to a collection, which

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.737
is this registry,
and defining some

00:03:09.737 --> 00:03:11.820
of the essential properties,
like we've done here.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.320
The last thing I'm
going to show you

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.370
is our Dataflow pipeline
that we've created.

00:03:18.362 --> 00:03:20.320
What we've done to build
this Dataflow pipeline

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.740
is we've included SDK client
libraries for Java and Python,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:24.921
plus REST APIs.

00:03:24.921 --> 00:03:26.920
And we've essentially
cloned the git repository.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.580
We've updated some files there.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.107
And then we deployed it
directly to Dataflow here.

00:03:31.107 --> 00:03:33.190
I'll show you here what
the pipeline we've created

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.970
looks like.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.700
So essentially, it's
reading messages,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.033
applying window and techniques
for streaming information

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.154
from our IoT devices, and it's
creating a table row and entity

00:03:42.154 --> 00:03:43.570
conversion so that
we can actually

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:47.420
push it into other sources
like BigQuery and Datastore.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.070
OK.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.160
Now that we've had this whole
pipeline set up, let's go ahead

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.690
into the last step and show
you what it looks like in terms

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.010
of the output in BigQuery.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.080
We have our data, event_data.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.420
And this is where all the
data coming out of Dataflow

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.700
gets pushed into this table.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.170
So we've created a data
set called IoT Airlines

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.247
v2 with our table events_data.

00:04:06.247 --> 00:04:08.830
And so you can see here this is
what the schema looks like it.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.750
It includes customer
ID, the event type,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.220
things like customer name.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.409
We also have how many
bags are checked,

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:16.450
how many seats are assigned
under their name, how

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.459
long the trip was,
what the origin is,

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:19.566
what the destination.

00:04:19.566 --> 00:04:21.190
Let's just do an
example really quickly

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.140
of how we can compose a
query on this data set.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.700
Certain routes might be
more likely than others

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.370
for certain upsells,
like, for example,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.870
if somebody's flying out at
6:00 in the morning on a Monday,

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:29.953
maybe its a business trip.

00:04:29.953 --> 00:04:31.810
You can offer them to buy Wi-Fi.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.090
You can also upsell them if
a customer's carrying bags

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:35.890
or has a connecting
flight, for example.

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.810
Maybe they want to upgrade
from coach to first class.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.530
So let's go ahead and just do
an example query of just pulling

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.659
customer emails, IDs,
and see if there's

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.200
anybody who has actually done
a bag check, for example, so we

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.790
can potentially upgrade them.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.570
So let's go ahead and open this.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.270
I have my example query here.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.850
And you just input it
directly into this query box

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.530
here and run the query.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.320
We were selecting customer
email, ID from this data set

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.310
and table event_data.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.020
And we're looking for customers
who have more than one bag,

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.840
and the trip leg
is more than one.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.380
And you can see here this
is just fake generated data.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.320
But in a real-case scenario,
we might have a different list

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.570
